<writing_rules>
ESSENTIAL:
- 1500-2000 words. First 20 words must contain the core premise.
- Complete, natural sentences. No fragments.
- Active voice. 12-18 words per sentence average.
- Vary sentence length: a 6-word sentence next to a 24-word sentence creates rhythm.
- Each paragraph = 3-5 sentences. No longer.
- Natural paragraph flow (NOT bullet points or fragments).

STRUCTURE:
- Hook at the start to grab attention
- Clear structure with intro, body, and conclusion
- Conversational and engaging for a 5-10 minute video
- End with a call to action asking viewers to like and subscribe
- Written in a style suitable for a YouTube video narration

NEVER:
- Direct quotes with quotation marks. Use narrated speech: "He admitted the plan was flawed."
- Parentheses, brackets, or dashes used as stage directions.
- Markdown formatting, bold, italics, or code blocks in the script body.
- Creator intros: "Hey guys", "Welcome back", "Today we are looking at".
- Filler transitions: "In conclusion", "To summarize", "As we can see".
- Rhetorical questions mid-script. Only the hook may be a question.
- Passive constructions. "The player discovers" not "It is discovered by the player."
- Audience address. "You" pulls focus. Keep the story about the game/characters.

FACTUAL ACCURACY:
- Every named entity must appear in the transcript. If a name appears only in lore context, flag it with a narrative qualifier: "According to the game's lore..."
- If you are uncertain about a specific number, date, or name, OMIT it entirely rather than guess.
- Do not add explanatory context that isn't in the source. The viewer has the video for visuals.
- Stay FACTUALLY accurate to the transcript - do not make up events or details

CHARACTERS:
- You MUST only mention verified characters from the context
- DO NOT invent or mention any character names that are NOT in the verified context
- DO NOT create fictional characters like "Sarah", "Mark", "David", "Alex" unless they appear in the transcript
- If you need to reference people, use generic terms like "a friend", "a character" or "the victim" instead of made-up names
- DO NOT invent relationships between characters - if the transcript doesn't explicitly state a connection between characters, do NOT imply or state that they have a relationship
- DO NOT make up roles for characters (e.g., don't say "Chief Bank is leading the inquiry" unless explicitly stated in transcript)
- Only describe characters and events that are explicitly mentioned in the transcript - do not infer or assume details not directly stated

TITLES:
- 6-10 words. Must reference a SPECIFIC detail from the transcript — not a generic theme.
- Structure variety across scripts: question ("What Made X Impossible?"), statement ("The One Rule That Broke Y"), contrast ("X Was Simple. Then Z Happened.").
- Avoid: "The [Noun] of [Noun]", all-caps words, clickbait that overpromises.
- No spoilers in the title unless the transcript is already public knowledge.

DESCRIPTION:
- 2-3 sentences. Open with a verb that differs from the previous script's description opener.
- Summarize the hook in a way that complements the title, not repeats it.
- End with 3-5 lowercase hashtags. Game name first.

TAGS:
- 10-15 comma-separated keywords, strictly lowercase.
- Include: game name, main characters/entities, core themes, narrative style.
</writing_rules>

<self_verification>
After writing, silently verify:
1. Every named entity exists in the transcript or verified context. Delete any that don't.
2. No sentence starts with "As", "While", or "Despite" (weak openings).
3. The title could only apply to THIS transcript, not any other video on the same game.
4. No two consecutive sentences have the same grammatical structure.
5. Removing the last sentence would make the script feel incomplete.
6. The script is 1500-2000 words.
7. The script flows naturally when read aloud.
</self_verification>

<source_material>
TRANSCRIPT:
{{ transcript }}
</source_material>

<output_format>
Output exactly:

TITLE: [6-10 words]
DESCRIPTION: [2-3 sentences, hashtags at end]
TAGS: [10-15 comma-separated lowercase keywords]

[Script body. Separated from TAGS by exactly one blank line. 1500-2000 words.]

Rules:
- No markdown code blocks around the output.
- No introductory text before TITLE:.
- No trailing text after the script body.
- The blank line between TAGS and the script body must contain exactly one newline character.
</output_format>